      "sequence_position": 7,
      "problem": "$\\,$ $n$  is prime and  $m$  is even.\n\nThe negation of the composite predicate above is the following:\n\n$\\,$ \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}{{field-3}}",
      "worked_solution": "De Morgan's law for conjunctions states that\n\n$$\n¬(A∧B)=¬A∨¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of \"$n$ is prime and $m$ is even\" is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(n\\text{ is prime and }m\\text{ is even}) & ≡¬(n\\text{ is prime}∧m\\text{ is even}) \\\\  & ≡¬(n\\text{ is prime})∨¬(m\\text{ is even}) \\\\  & ≡n\\text{ is not prime}∨m\\text{ is odd} \\\\  & ≡n\\text{ is not prime or }m\\text{ is odd}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
